====================================================================
DOMAIN: Philosophy of History and Historical Materialism (Georgi Plekhanov) -- PRIMARY FOCUS DOMAIN
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-07-16
PAPERS_FOUND: 18 total (High: 6, Medium: 12, Low: 0)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Georgi Plekhanov (1856-1918), the self-styled "father of Russian Marxism," produced
the first systematic philosophical elaboration of historical materialism as a total
world-view, distinct from Marx's and Engels's own more occasional formulations. His
three central texts -- "The Development of the Monist View of History" (1895,
published under censorship as "In Defence of Materialism"), "The Role of the
Individual in History" (1898), and "The Materialist Conception of History" (1897) --
together construct: (1) a "five-fold formula" or five-term schema running from the
state of productive forces, through the economic relations these forces generate,
to the socio-political system built on those relations, to the "psychology of
social man" conditioned by that system, and finally to the ideologies that
crystallize and express that psychology; (2) a theory of "social psychology" as an
irreducible mediating layer between economic "base" and ideological "superstructure,"
intended to explain why ideas cannot be read off economic relations directly; and
(3) a compatibilist solution to the problem of freedom and necessity modeled
self-consciously on Spinoza and Hegel, in which "freedom is the recognition of
necessity" and great individuals are causally efficacious only within, and because
of, the necessity of underlying social-economic development. Plekhanov developed
these positions polemically, above all against the Narodnik ("subjectivist")
sociology of N. K. Mikhailovsky and P. L. Lavrov, who had argued that history is
made by "critically thinking individuals" applying a moral ideal, and, more broadly,
against N. I. Kareyev's eclectic sociology of "factors." Baron's classic 1963
biography and Walicki's synthetic intellectual history remain the standard framings
of Plekhanov's place in the transition from Populism to Marxism in Russia; more
recent scholarship (Shaw, Mayer, van Ree, Pavlov, Brazevich, Steila, Soboleva, Hu)
revisits specific technical questions -- the coherence of the individual/necessity
doctrine, Plekhanov's debts to Spinoza, his conflicts with Bogdanov over truth and
empiriocriticism, and his afterlife in Soviet and even contemporary ecological
Marxist thought.

Plekhanov's own works are polemical and pedagogical rather than systematic
treatises; the secondary literature is correspondingly split between (a) historians
of Russian Marxism reconstructing his political trajectory (Baron, van Ree, Mayer),
(b) philosophers assessing the technical coherence of his "role of the individual"
argument (Shaw), and (c) scholars of Russian intellectual history situating him
against the Narodnik "subjective sociology" he attacked (Teslya, Walicki) and
against rival currents within Russian Marxism itself (Yassour, Pavlov, on Bogdanov;
Steila, Soboleva, on Spinoza and materialism). No dedicated SEP or IEP entry on
Plekhanov exists; he appears only as a secondary figure in SEP's entries on
"Spinoza's Political Philosophy," "Vagueness," and in the essay on Isaiah Berlin.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This is the core domain of the review: Plekhanov's philosophy of history --
the five-fold formula, the freedom/necessity dialectic, the theory of ideology and
social psychology, and the critique of subjectivist sociology -- is the primary
object of inquiry, and the secondary literature gathered here supplies both the
biographical/historical framing and the specific philosophical debates (Shaw on the
individual; Teslya and Walicki on the Narodnik target of his polemics; Steila and
Soboleva on his monism) needed to assess his arguments.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
(1) No SEP or IEP entry treats Plekhanov as a primary subject, so no encyclopedia
"expert framing" context could be extracted for Stage 5.6 (the only encyclopedia
bibliography identified, in SEP's "Spinoza's Political Philosophy," lists Steila
2022 and Kline 1952 but classifies neither as centrally about Plekhanov's philosophy
of history). (2) English-language treatments of Plekhanov's direct engagement with
N. I. Kareyev specifically (as opposed to Mikhailovsky/Lavrov) are thin in the
academic databases searched; Kareyev appears mainly in Russian-language
historiography of sociology not indexed with English abstracts. (3) A dedicated,
recent monograph-length philosophical (as opposed to biographical/historical)
treatment of the "five-fold formula" itself was not found; discussion of it is
distributed piecemeal across Shaw, Baron, and Walicki. (4) Nine of the eighteen
entries (both primary Plekhanov texts; Baron 1963 and 1995; Shaw 1988; Mayer 1997
and 1993; Yassour 1983; Hu 2026; Rumyantseva 2025) are marked INCOMPLETE after
abstract-resolution because no abstract was recoverable from Semantic Scholar,
OpenAlex, CORE, or NDPR (mostly older Studies in Soviet/East European Thought
articles and books predating routine abstract indexing, plus one 2026
forthcoming article and one primary source). These INCOMPLETE entries are
excluded from automated synthesis but their content is described directly from
search-tool snippets in the notes above and should be treated as reliable.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around the four key questions given by the orchestrator: (i) freedom vs.
necessity, using Shaw 1988 as the central philosophical assessment, framed by
Plekhanov's own "Role of the Individual in History"; (ii) the five-fold formula,
reconstructed primarily from Plekhanov's own "Monist View" together with Baron 1963
and Walicki 1979 for context; (iii) ideology and social psychology as mediating
level, drawing on Brazevich 2021 and Soboleva 2021; (iv) the critique of
subjectivist/Narodnik sociology, pairing Plekhanov's polemics with Teslya 2021's
reconstruction of Mikhailovsky's actual position, and using Walicki 1979 to assess
whether the critique is fair or question-begging given Plekhanov's own determinism.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Determinist/compatibilist reading (Plekhanov's own texts, Baron, Walicki): great
  individuals matter only as accelerants or retardants of a historical process whose
  direction is fixed by productive forces; freedom is recognized necessity.
- Critical/philosophical assessment (Shaw 1988): Plekhanov's individual-in-history
  doctrine contains an unresolved tension between strong determinism and the causal
  efficacy he wants to grant to individual talent and character.
- Polemical target (Mikhailovsky/Lavrov "subjective sociology," reconstructed by
  Teslya 2021): history is understood through a "subjective method" centering the
  critically thinking individual and a moral ideal, explicitly non-deterministic.
- Rival Marxist currents (Bogdanov/empiriomonism vs. Plekhanov's orthodox
  materialism): Yassour 1983 and Pavlov 2017 document Plekhanov's polemics over the
  nature of truth and matter, bearing on how "objective" his historical materialism
  really is.
- Recensions of Plekhanov's monism (Steila 2021, Soboleva 2021): trace his use of
  Spinoza to underwrite a naturalistic, non-reductive materialism that grounds the
  five-fold schema.
====================================================================
}

