  POSITION: Historical reconstruction of Plekhanov's theory of Russian
  historical development as the foundation of Menshevik stagism.
  },
  keywords = {two-stage-theory, Russian-development, historical-materialism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{baron1981warrevolution,
  author = {Baron, Samuel H.},
  title = {Plekhanov in War and Revolution, 1914--17},
  journal = {International Review of Social History},
  year = {1981},
  volume = {26},
  number = {3},
  pages = {325--376},
  doi = {10.1017/S0020859000007215},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Baron traces Plekhanov's political conduct from the outbreak
  of World War I through the February and October Revolutions of 1917,
  documenting his adoption of "defencist" support for Russia's war effort, his
  consequent break with the Zimmerwald-aligned internationalist wing of Russian
  and European socialism, and his political isolation during 1917.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the project's question of how Plekhanov's WWI
  defencism is to be understood against his earlier internationalist Marxism;
  as a detailed archival study by Plekhanov's principal biographer, it supplies
  the empirical backbone for any account of the coherence (or lack of it)
  between his wartime patriotism and his prior theoretical commitments.

  POSITION: Biographical-historical reconstruction of Plekhanov's defencism and
  its reception, largely from a standpoint sympathetic to treating it as
  continuous with his prior political trajectory rather than as rupture.
  },
  keywords = {WWI-defencism, internationalism, Zimmerwald, biography, High},
  abstract = {Despite the fact that the views of Georgii Valentinovich Plekhanov (1856–1918) on the World War cannot be studied in his voluminous collected works – the editor abruptly terminated the edition with an article Plekhanov published on the eve of the war's outbreak – his position is broadly familiar to students of Russian and international socialism. Thanks to his substantial published writings from September 1914 to March 1917 scattered through the press of at least five countries, a two volume collection of his articles and speeches for the remainder of 1917, and several brief secondary accounts, it is possible to trace Plekhanov's wartime outlook. By contrast, little is known of his political initiatives, associations and conflicts during the war years. A considerable share of his correspondence and other personal papers – the kind of material likely to illuminate these aspects – has been printed by the Dom Plekhanova , the Leningrad repository that holds almost all of them.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{gaido2017iskragroup,
  author = {Gaido, Daniel and Jozami, Maximiliano},
  title = {El grupo Iskra y el "modelo leninista" de organización partidaria: Un debate en el seno de la socialdemocracia rusa},
  journal = {Izquierdas},
  year = {2017},
  number = {35},
  pages = {318--369},
  doi = {10.4067/s0718-50492017000400318},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Drawing on primary documents and Vera Zasulich's 1902 critique
  of Socialist-Revolutionary terrorism, the authors argue that the
  organizational model of Lenin's What Is to Be Done? (1902) was not simply an
  individual initiative but the collective project of the Iskra editorial
  board, adapting German Social Democratic practice to Russian conditions and
  to factional struggle against "Economists," the Bund, and local
  organizations, and that this same model of centralized party organization
  precipitated the 1903 Bolshevik/Menshevik split at the Second Congress of the
  RSDLP.

  RELEVANCE: Gives the organizational prehistory of Plekhanov's break with
  Lenin: Plekhanov sat on the Iskra editorial board and supported the
  centralizing project before siding with Martov's minority against Lenin's
  party-rules proposal at the Second Congress, so this paper supplies the
  concrete institutional sequence underlying the project's question of why the
  split occurred and how it maps onto rival theories of the party.

  POSITION: Historical-materialist reconstruction crediting the Iskra board
  collectively, not Lenin alone, with the "Leninist" model of party
  organization, and tracing its bifurcation into Bolshevik and Menshevik
  variants.
  },
  keywords = {Iskra, RSDLP-split, party-organization, Menshevism, High},
  abstract = {espanolEn marzo de 1902 Lenin publico un opusculo titulado ?Que hacer? Problemas candentes de nuestro movimiento, en el cual propuso un modelo de organizacion partidaria para el naciente movimiento socialdemocrata ruso, que debia organizarse a nivel nacional. Dicho libro ha planteado historicamente una serie de interrogantes, de los cuales los mas importantes son los siguientes: ?Estaba Lenin proponiendo un nuevo modelo de organizacion partidaria (un “partido de cuadros” en contraposicion a un “partido de masas”) o simplemente adaptando el ejemplo del Partido Socialdemocrata de Alemania (SPD, el “partido-guia” de la Segunda Internacional) a las condiciones de la clandestinidad rusa y de la lucha fraccional contra los “economistas”, el Bund judio y una serie de organizaciones locales? ?Representaba dicho modelo de organizacion partidaria una iniciativa individual de Lenin, o era el proyecto conjunto del grupo que editaba el periodico Iskra (La chispa), que venia publicandose desde el primero de diciembre de 1900 y que organizaria el Segundo Congreso del Partido Obrero Socialdemocrata de Rusia (POSDR) en julio-agosto de 1903? Finalmente: ?Que relacion existe entre el modelo de organizacion partidaria propuesto en el libro de Lenin y la subsecuente escision entre bolcheviques y mencheviques en el Segundo Congreso del POSDR? En el presente articulo intentaremos responder a estas preguntas a la luz de una serie de documentos primarios recientemente traducidos del ruso, asi como de la critica de Vera Zasulich, una de las representantes mas destacadas del grupo editorial de Iskra, a la tactica del terrorismo individual empleada por el recientemente creado Partido Socialista Revolucionario ruso, critica que fue publicada en el organo teorico de la Socialdemocracia alemana, Die neue Zeit, en diciembre de 1902. EnglishIn March 1902, Lenin published a book entitled What Is to Be Done? Burning Questions of Our Movement, proposing a model of party organization for the nascent Russian social democratic movement, which had to be organized nationally. The book has historically raised a number of questions, of which the most important are: Was Lenin proposing a new model of party organization (a "cadre party" as opposed to a "mass party") or simply adapting the example of the Social Democratic Party of Germany (SPD, the backbone of the Second International) to the conditions of Russian autocracy and to the factional struggle against the "economists", the Jewish Bund and a series of local organizations? Did that model of party organization represent an individual initiative of Lenin, or was it the joint project of the group that published the newspaper Iskra (Spark), which had been appearing since the first of December 1900 and which would organize the Second Congress of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party (RSDLP) in July-August 1903? Finally, what was the connection between the model of party organization proposed in Lenin’s book and the subsequent split between Bolsheviks and Mensheviks at the Second Congress of the RSDLP? In this article we will try to answer these questions in the light of a number of primary documents recently translated from Russian as well as of the criticism by Vera Zasulich, one of the most prominent representatives of the Iskra editorial board, of the tactics of individual terrorism employed by the newly created Russian Socialist Revolutionary Party, a criticism published in the theoretical organ of the German Social Democracy, Die neue Zeit, in December 1902.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{avila2017kautsky,
  author = {Avila, Paula and Gaido, Daniel},
  title = {Karl Kautsky and the Russian Revolution of 1905: A Debate on the Driving Forces and the Prospects of the Russian Revolution in the Second International},
  journal = {International Critical Thought},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {7},
  number = {3},
  pages = {402--417},
  doi = {10.1080/21598282.2017.1323312},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: In October 1906 Plekhanov circulated a questionnaire to
  leaders of international Social Democracy asking about the class character
  of the 1905 Russian Revolution and the tactics it implied; twelve replies
  overwhelmingly endorsed the Menshevik view that the revolution was bourgeois
  (at most bourgeois-with-socialist-elements) and that cooperation with liberal
  opposition parties was therefore warranted, but Kautsky's reply rejected any
  coalition with the bourgeoisie and so undercut the Menshevik position from
  within the very poll Plekhanov had organized to confirm it.

  RELEVANCE: This is a direct primary-source reconstruction of Plekhanov
  soliciting theoretical validation for the two-stage schema and instead
  provoking a consequential internal challenge to it; it shows the "two-stage"
  theory being tested and contested in real time by the leading theorists of
  the Second International, bearing directly on the project's question of how
  the philosophy of history shaped Plekhanov's theory of Russian revolution.

  POSITION: Documents the Kautsky/Menshevik disagreement as an early fracture
  in the stagist consensus, later exploited by both Lenin and Trotsky for
  divergent theoretical purposes.
  },
  keywords = {two-stage-theory, Kautsky, Second-International, 1905-revolution, High},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT In October 1906 G. V. Plekhanov sent a questionnaire to the leaders of international Social Democracy, requesting their opinion about the class character of the Russian Revolution and the tactics which followed from this analysis for the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party. He received 12 responses, which overwhelmingly supported the Menshevik position: the Russian Revolution was not socialist, at most it was bourgeois revolution with socialist elements; the boycott of the Duma was considered an error and cooperation with the parties of the bourgeois opposition, essential. The exception to this rule was the reply of Karl Kautsky. While Kautsky’s answer was cautious enough for both Lenin and Trotsky to consider it as a confirmation of their theories, it categorically rejected the possibility of a coalition with the bourgeoisie, the posture favoured by Plekhanov, and therefore constituted a clear refutation of the Menshevik prospects on the Russian Revolution.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@incollection{day2009permanentrevolution,
  author = {Day, Richard B.},
  title = {Introduction -- The Historical Origin of the Expression `Permanent Revolution'},
  booktitle = {Witnesses to Permanent Revolution: The Documentary Record},
  year = {2009},
  pages = {1--58},
  publisher = {Brill},
  doi = {10.1163/EJ.9789004167704.I-684.5},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Day's introduction to this documentary collection traces the
  genealogy of "permanent revolution" as a technical term from Marx and Engels
  through the Second International debates to Trotsky and Parvus, situating it
  as a rival schema to the Menshevik/Plekhanovite two-stage theory of Russian
  revolution that separated a bourgeois-democratic phase from a subsequent
  socialist one.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the comparative frame the project's key questions
  require for assessing Plekhanov's two-stage schema: by reconstructing the
  rival "permanent revolution" tradition documentarily, it lets Menshevik
  stagism be read as one contested position among several live options within
  Russian and international Marxism rather than as an uncontroversial
  deduction from historical materialism.

  POSITION: Editorial/historiographical introduction favoring the documentary
  reconstruction of the permanent-revolution tradition as a serious theoretical
  rival to stagism, rather than adjudicating between them.
  },
  keywords = {permanent-revolution, two-stage-theory, Trotsky, historiography, Medium},
  abstract = {This is the introduction chapter of the book, which reveals an array of opinions ranging from Plekhanov's conviction that a bourgeois revolution was pending-even if it must be led by the workers-to the opposite position shared by Ryazanov, Parvus and Trotsky, namely, that a permanent revolution would rapidly point beyond bourgeois democracy in the direction of socialism. Marxism emerged in Russia in a struggle against revolutionary Narodnik populism, but during and after the 1905 revolution the echoes of previous disputes with the Narodniks were still apparent in assessments of Russia's 'peculiar' characteristics given by Ryazanov, Trotsky, and even Lenin. Plekhanov and Lenin succeeded in denying the Bor'ba group any official representation at the Second Congress of the Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party, which was held in London and Brussels in the summer of 1903. This congress resulted in the famous split between Bolsheviks and Mensheviks over organisational issues. Keywords: bourgeois revolution; David Ryazanov; Georgy Plekhanov; Lenin; Leon Trotsky; Marxism; Narodnik populism; Parvus; permanent revolution; Russian Social-Democratic Labour Party},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{thatcher2021momentoftruth,
  author = {Thatcher, Ian D.},
  title = {The 1905--07 Russian Revolution as a `Moment of Truth': An Overlooked Contribution from Menshevism},
  journal = {Revolutionary Russia},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {34},
  number = {2},
  pages = {175--195},
  doi = {10.1080/09546545.2021.1984064},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Thatcher recovers a largely overlooked, projected five-part
  Menshevik collective study of the social forces active in the 1905
  Revolution (four of five volumes appeared in 1907, covering reaction, the
  proletariat, the peasantry, and the liberal/democratic bourgeoisie), arguing
  it represents the first sustained attempt from within Russian Marxism to
  analyze the revolution as a whole from a single (Menshevik) perspective, and
  that it anticipates interpretations later developed in the more famous
  1909--14 Menshevik history of the same period.

  RELEVANCE: Provides evidence for how Menshevik theorists (not Plekhanov
  alone) operationalized the two-stage schema empirically, class by class, in
  analyzing 1905 -- material the project can use to assess how far "Menshevism"
  followed distinctively from Plekhanov's own theory as opposed to a broader
  collective Menshevik research program.

  POSITION: Recovers an internal Menshevik body of empirical class analysis as
  a serious and continuous contribution to Russian Marxist historiography of
  1905, rather than treating Menshevik theory as static doctrine.
  },
  keywords = {Menshevism, 1905-revolution, class-analysis, Medium},
  abstract = {This article is the first exposition of a projected five-part Menshevik study of social forces in the Russian Revolution of 1905, only four volumes of which appeared in 1907 covering reaction, the proletariat, the peasantry, and the liberal and democratic bourgeoisie. This collective effort marked perhaps the first attempt to present an overall analysis of the revolution from within one perspective, that of the Menshevik variety of Russian Marxism. Despite the centrality of perceptions of revolution to participants and future historians of Russian socialism and of 1905, this project has been largely overlooked. This is to be regretted, for the volumes contain interpretations now familiar on the nature of the 1905 revolution and why it failed. Furthermore, there is continuity between the works and authors of 1907 and the subsequent (1909–14) much more famous Menshevik history of social movements in Russia at the beginning of the twentieth century.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{gaido2015zasulich,
  author = {Gaido, Daniel and Bosch Alessio, Constanza Daniela},
  title = {Vera Zasulich's Critique of Neo-Populism: Party Organisation and Individual Terrorism in the Russian Revolutionary Movement (1878--1902)},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2015},
  volume = {23},
  number = {4},
  pages = {93--125},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206X-12341441},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The authors reconstruct Vera Zasulich's trajectory from her
  1878 assassination attempt on Governor Trepov, through her role as one of the
  five founders of the Emancipation of Labour group in 1883, to her 1902
  polemic against the newly formed Socialist-Revolutionary Party's revival of
  individual-terrorist tactics -- a polemic that supported the organizational
  plans for the RSDLP that Lenin was developing in What Is to Be Done?.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the Emancipation of Labour group's founding polemic
  (against Narodnik individual terrorism, in favor of organized mass party
  politics) as the shared starting point from which Plekhanov's later
  disagreement with Lenin over the *form* of that same organizational project
  emerged; useful background for the project's question of how the 1903 split
  relates to Plekhanov's own founding commitments.

  POSITION: Situates Zasulich (and by extension the Emancipation of Labour
  group) as originally aligned with, not opposed to, the centralizing
  organizational logic Lenin later formalized -- complicating any simple
  narrative in which Menshevism was anti-organizational from the outset.
  },
  keywords = {Emancipation-of-Labour, party-organization, Narodism, Medium},
  abstract = {Vera Zasulich’s shooting of Trepov, a governor of St Petersburg who had ordered the flogging of a political prisoner, in January 1878, catapulted her to international fame as a revolutionary heroine, a reputation that she put to good use by becoming one of the five ‘founding parents’ of Russian Marxism that created the ‘Group for the Emancipation of Labour’ in 1883. But her act of self-sacrifice also triggered, to her dismay, the institutionalisation of individual-terrorist tactics in the Russian Populist movement with the creation of the ‘People’s Will’ (Narodnaya Volya) Party in 1879. The organisation went into decline after the killing of Tsar Alexander II in 1881, and Populism itself was increasingly superseded by Marxism as the hegemonic force on the left with the rise of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party (RSDLP). But individual-terrorist tactics reappeared with the creation of the Socialist Revolutionary Party in 1902, prompting Zasulich to write an article for Die neue Zeit, the theoretical organ of German Social Democracy, in which she both condemned the Neo-Populist tendency as deleterious to the rising labour movement and supported the organisational plans for the RSDLP sponsored by the Iskra group, developed at length by Lenin in his book What Is to Be Done?, published in March 1902. This article provides the background to Vera Zasulich’s article ‘The Terrorist Tendency in Russia’ (December 1902), setting it against the history of the Russian revolutionary movement from 1878 to 1902.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{ascher1984tiutiukinreview,
  author = {Ascher, Abraham},
  title = {Pervaia rossiiskaia revoliutsiia i G. V. Plekhanov: Iz istorii ideinoi bor'by v rabochem dvizhenii Rossii v 1905--1907 gg.},
  journal = {The American Historical Review},
  year = {1984},
  volume = {89},
  number = {1},
  pages = {172},
  doi = {10.2307/1856027},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This review by the leading Western historian of Menshevism
  assesses S. V. Tiutiukin's Soviet-era monograph on the ideological struggles
  within the Russian workers' movement during the 1905--07 revolution,
  reporting Tiutiukin's account of Plekhanov's positions and his conflicts with
  rival tendencies (Bolshevik and Narodnik-descended) within the revolutionary
  movement of that period.

  RELEVANCE: Offers rare access, via a specialist Western reviewer, to a
  Soviet-historiographical treatment of Plekhanov's role in 1905 that is
  otherwise available only in Russian; useful for triangulating how Soviet and
  Western scholarship diverge in narrating Plekhanov's positions during the
  first revolution, feeding the project's broader interest in how his
  political trajectory has been received and contested.

  POSITION: A Western Menshevik-historiography perspective (Ascher) evaluating
  a Soviet-orthodox monograph (Tiutiukin) on Plekhanov's 1905 politics.
  },
  keywords = {1905-revolution, Soviet-historiography, book-review, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{elwood2011brussels,
  author = {Elwood, Carter},
  title = {Lenin and the Brussels `Unity' Conference of July 1914},
  booktitle = {The Non-Geometric Lenin},
  year = {2011},
  pages = {73--86},
  publisher = {Anthem Press},
  doi = {10.7135/UPO9780857288295.006},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Elwood examines the International Socialist Bureau's July
  1914 Brussels "Unity" conference, the last major pre-war attempt by the
  Second International to reconcile the Bolshevik and Menshevik factions of
  Russian Social Democracy, and Lenin's tactics in evading and ultimately
  defeating this unification effort just weeks before the outbreak of World
  War I.

  RELEVANCE: Documents the final failed attempt at organizational reunification
  before the 1903 split hardened irreversibly and before the war reshaped every
  faction's politics -- relevant to the project's question of how deep and how
  final the Lenin/Plekhanov organizational break actually was on the eve of
  1914, immediately before defencism reconfigured the political landscape
  again.

  POSITION: Treats the RSDLP split as an ongoing, actively contested
  organizational problem for the Second International rather than a settled
  fact after 1903, with Lenin as the chief obstacle to reunification.
  },
  keywords = {RSDLP-split, party-unity, Second-International, Medium},
  abstract = {‘The most important thing is to prove that only we are the party… Unless they accept our conditions, there will be no rapprochement’. Alternatively, ‘agree to nothing, walk out, promise to submit the “counter-proposals” of our dear comrades to our own congress’ which will complete the task of building an all-Bolshevik Social Democratic Party. With these uncompromising words of wisdom Lenin sent Inessa Armand off to do battle with G. V. Plekhanov, L. D. Trotsky, P. B. Aksel'rod and I. O. Martov at the ‘Unity’ Conference which the International Socialist Bureau (ISB) called in a last vain attempt to unify the factious groups of Russian Social Democracy. The 28 delegates who attended this three-day meeting in Brussels were impressed neither by Armand's reluctant presentation nor by Lenin's vitriolic arguments. Indeed, Bolshevik conduct in Brussels served only to unify the disparate non-Bolshevik groups and to ensure them the firm support of Karl Kautsky, Emile Vandervelde, Camille Huysmans and the other influential leaders of the Socialist (Second) International. As a result of the conference, the Tenth Congress of the International, scheduled to convene in Vienna on 23 August 1914, very likely would have witnessed the expulsion of the Bolsheviks from the European socialist movement. The outbreak of the First World War, however, saved Lenin this indignity. It also served for many years to obscure from historical scrutiny the events which transpired in Brussels and the potential consequences of the Bolshevik defeat.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@inproceedings{zhuravleva2019theoretical,
  author = {Zhuravleva, G. P. and Kornilova, I. M. and Kovnir, V. N. and Koroleva, E. I. and Cherednichenko, O. V.},
  title = {Theoretical Views and Social Democratic Activities of G.V. Plekhanov},
  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on "Humanities and Social Sciences: Novations, Problems, Prospects" (HSSNPP 2019)},
  year = {2019},
  publisher = {Atlantis Press},
  doi = {10.2991/HSSNPP-19.2019.14},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This conference paper surveys Plekhanov's political career in
  three stages -- his early attraction to Narodism, his conversion to Marxism
  after reading the Communist Manifesto, and his subsequent cooperation with,
  and eventual estrangement from, Lenin that pushed him toward the Mensheviks
  -- as a broad biographical overview rather than an original archival or
  interpretive contribution.

  RELEVANCE: Offers a compact, low-specificity summary of the Narodism ->
  Marxism -> Menshevism trajectory that can serve as an orienting overview
  alongside the more detailed and archivally grounded studies in this domain,
  though it does not itself advance a distinct interpretive thesis.

  POSITION: General biographical survey without a strong interpretive stance
  on the split or on Menshevism's relation to Plekhanov's theory.
  },
  keywords = {biography, overview, Narodism, Low},
  abstract = {Theoretical views of G.V. Plekhanov on the future of mankind still evokes undoubted interest. As an encyclopedic personality himself, he brought up a large pleiad of brilliant minds who successfully represented Russia in various fields of knowledge economics, philosophy, literature and art. And now, in the modern world, his ideas do not age. In this regard, the purpose of the study is to analyze the life of the great man, his views, doubts, throwing, position. The main methods of research are the method of scientific abstraction, system analysis, deduction, the method of information structuring, the analysis of archival materials. The pedigree of the Plekhanov family is represented, the three stages of the Plekhanov’s path as a political figure are traced, Plekhanov's views on the development of social and political thought are examined. Plekhanov’s articles, as well as his actions, have always been vigorously discussed. He was fond of the ideas of Narodism, but there was a split among the Narodniks. After reading the Manifesto of the Communist Party, he was carried away by Marxism and abandoned the ideas of populism. He began to cooperate with Lenin, but the disagreements between them led Plekhanov to the side of the Mensheviks. In Plekhanov’s works, undoubtedly, there is something that is considered valuable and durable, and views on the future of a wise, highly educated and just man can be traced. Keywords—theorist, thinker, populism, Marxism, ideology},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{savin2020origins,
  author = {Savin, Alexey E.},
  title = {Origins of the Interpretation and Criticism of Philosophical Foundations of Leninism in Western Marxism},
  journal = {Vestnik Tomskogo gosudarstvennogo universiteta},
  year = {2020},
  number = {458},
  pages = {79--84},
  doi = {10.17223/15617793/458/9},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs the origins of "left communist" (Raetekommunismus) criticism of Leninism in 1920s-30s Germany, Holland, and Denmark, centered on Anton Pannekoek's "Lenin as Philosopher," a sustained critique of Lenin's "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism." The critique holds that Lenin's (and, behind him, Plekhanov's) natural-scientific materialism is a bourgeois-Enlightenment inheritance rather than genuine historical materialism, and traces how this reading was transmitted to the Frankfurt School chiefly via Herbert Marcuse's "Soviet Marxism" (1958), becoming the standard Western-left framing of Soviet dialectical materialism.

  RELEVANCE: Documents the specific genealogy by which the philosophical materialism codified by Plekhanov and adopted by Lenin became, for council communists and later the Frankfurt School, the paradigmatic target of Western Marxist anti-Leninism, directly answering the domain's question about Plekhanov's reception and critique within Western Marxism.

  POSITION: Intellectual-historical reconstruction of the council-communist/Frankfurt School critique of Leninist (and by extension Plekhanovite) philosophical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {Western-Marxism-reception, Lenin, Pannekoek, Frankfurt-School, High},
  abstract = {The aim of the article is to discover the nature of the widespread criticism of Leninism in Western countries in the "left communism" or "communism of the Soviets" (Raetekommunismus), which arose in Germany, Holland, and Denmark in the 1920s and 1930s. To understand the general lines of the criticism of the philosophy of Leninism the author analyzes the ideas presented in the work Lenin as Philosopher by Anton Pannekoek, one of the greatest thinkers and politicians of the "communism of the Soviets". In its philosophical part, the work is devoted to the criticism of Lenin's main philosophical work Materialism and Empirio-Criticism. The author also takes into account the articles devoted to this criticism by Karl Korsch and Paul Mattik, other founders of "communism of the Soviets". The significance of these works is determined by the fact that they constitute the philosophical foundation of contemporary Western "Marxist anti-Leninism". The author reveals the political presuppositions and the political background of the polemic about the philosophical foundations of Leninism. The background is a polemic about the significance of the Russian revolution and the principles of building the Bolshevik party for the rest of the world and especially for Western countries and their Communist parties. The philosophical polemic with Leninism grows out of a doubt about the universal significance of the experience of the Russian revolution. In particular, Pannekoek and Korsch put forward the thesis of the bourgeois-democratic, not socialist character of the Russian revolution. From this thesis, they conclude that the theoretical basis of the Russian revolution is also of a bourgeois character, i.e. the Russian revolution is based on the ideas of the Enlightenment. The philosophical foundation of the Enlightenment is natural-scientific materialism, not historical materialism, i.e. not Marxism. The article demonstrates the genesis of the concept of Leninism as (1) an anti-democratic tendency in the contemporary liberation movement, (2) an instrument for legitimizing the repressive practices of the bureaucracy in the workers' parties and in the "catching-up" states of organized capitalism, (3) a naturalistic mishmash of natural-scientific and historical materialism, ultimately suppressing and emasculating the historicity of Marxist thought. The author reveals how this concept was transmitted to modern Western left-wing thought through the Frankfurt school, and especially through Marcuse's work Soviet Marxism (1958), which for many years became the most popular theoretical source for the Marxist criticism of Soviet dialectical materialism in the Western left.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{pateman2019dogmatism,
  author = {Pateman, Joe},
  title = {Lenin without Dogmatism},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {71},
  number = {2},
  pages = {99--117},
  doi = {10.1007/s11212-019-09325-6},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Challenges the standard charge that Lenin's epistemology in "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism" grounds the dogmatism and intolerance of twentieth-century Marxism-Leninism. Through textual analysis, Pateman argues Lenin defends a fallibilist conception of scientific knowledge -- affirming uncertainty in natural, philosophical, and social science -- that is inconsistent with the "vulgarizer" caricature routinely applied to him.

  RELEVANCE: Directly engages the reception-and-critique question at the center of this domain by contesting the widely repeated charge (originating partly in Western Marxist and liberal critiques of the Plekhanov-Lenin materialist lineage) that the reflection-theoretic epistemology inherited from Plekhanov necessarily produces dogmatic Marxism-Leninism.

  POSITION: Revisionist defense of Lenin's (and implicitly Plekhanov's) epistemological legacy against the Western critical consensus represented by Pannekoek, Acton, and Kolakowski.
  },
  keywords = {Lenin, epistemology, reflection-theory, reception-debate, Medium},
  abstract = {A longstanding criticism of Lenin is that his epistemological contributions to the theory of scientific socialism prompted the decline of Marxism in dogmatism and despotism in the twentieth century. According to this narrative, Lenin claimed to possess the objective truth, and he therefore refused to tolerate alternative perspectives. This article subjects these claims to a textual analysis, and it argues that they are erroneous. Lenin defends a fallibilist account of science that affirms the uncertainty of knowledge in the natural, philosophical and social sciences. This fallibilism pervades the text that supposedly showcases his intolerance, Materialism and Empirio-Criticism. Lenin's scientific socialism opposes rigid conceptions of socialism, and it encourages an atmosphere of democratic debate. Whilst Lenin's writings have their flaws, they are not the founding documents of Marxist dogmatism that they are often portrayed as being. Lenin is not the narrow-minded vulgariser of Marxism that he is routinely taken to be.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{savino2018italian,
  author = {Savino, Giovanni},
  title = {Italian Marxism and Russian Socialism: Labriola, Gramsci and Philosophical and Cultural Questions of Revolutionary Movement (1890--1937)},
  journal = {Philosophy. Journal of the Higher School of Economics},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {II},
  number = {2},
  pages = {99--117},
  doi = {10.17323/2587-8719-2018-ii-2-99-117},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Analyzes the points of contact between Italian Marxist theorists (Antonio Labriola, Antonio Gramsci) and Russian Socialist/Communist figures (Plekhanov, Lenin, Trotsky). Argues that Labriola was a significant theoretical authority for Russian Social Democrats abroad, that his rejection of positivism and reformism within the Italian Socialist Party influenced debates in other European socialist parties, and that Gramsci's concept of praxis and rejection of positivism derive from Labriola while also engaging Russian Marxism.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies the principal evidence for comparing Plekhanov's philosophical orientation with Labriola's anti-positivist "philosophy of praxis," showing that the connection between the two traditions ran substantially through Labriola's broader influence on Russian emigre Social Democracy rather than through direct textual engagement between Plekhanov and Labriola themselves.

  POSITION: Comparative intellectual history situating Plekhanov within the wider Second International debate over positivism and historical materialism that also produced Labriola's and Gramsci's philosophy of praxis.
  },
  keywords = {Labriola, Second-International, Gramsci, comparative, High},
  abstract = {The aim of this paper is to discuss and analyze the relationship between Italian Marxist thinkers Antonio Labriola and Antonio Gramsci on the one side and prominent representatives of the Russian Socialist and Communist movement on the other: Georgiy Plekhanov, Vladimir Lenin, and Leon Trotsky. In the history of the Socialist (and Anarchic) movement in Italy and in Russia from the end of 19th to the beginning of 20th century, there are many reasons for a more careful study of the points of contact and coincidence between two countries: first of all, because of the existence of the Italian Socialist Party influential leaders of Russian origin, such as Anna Kulisheva (known in Italy as Anna Kuliscioff) and Angelica Balabanova (Angelica Balabanoff). Italian philosopher Antonio Labriola was a significant moral and theoretical authority in the intellectual formation of the Russian socialistic democrats abroad. The influence of Labriola on the European labor movement and on the debate on historical materialism was enormous, and his rejection of the positivism and reformism in the Italian Socialist Party also had an influence on the discussions in the European socialist parties. Antonio Gramsci, the founder of the Italian Communist Party, assimilated the concept of praxis theory, as well as the rejection of positivism and the reformism of Italian socialism from Labriola; Gramsci's idea of the creative power of the working class in the revolutionary process derives from Labriola's arguments about classes in historical materialism. Gramsci's interest in Labriola is associated not only with the role of the philosopher in the creation of the theory of praxis, or his teaching on historical materialism, but also with his influence on Russian Marxism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{bonilla2020reflexion,
  author = {Bonilla Bonilla, Manuel Alejandro},
  title = {La otra teoría de la reflexión de Lenin},
  journal = {Verinotio -- Revista on-line de Filosofia e Ciências Humanas},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {26},
  number = {1},
  pages = {263--277},
  doi = {10.36638/1981-061x.2020.v26.481},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the theory of reflection Lenin developed in his 1915 commentaries on Hegel's "Science of Logic," distinct from the reflection theory of "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism" (1908). In the later notebooks, Lenin presents reflection as an adaptive, reciprocal relation between the movement of knowledge and the movement of reality, in which reflection is a (mental) moment of the human process of objectification that transforms reality and constitutes the human being -- a more dialectical formulation than the earlier copy-theory epistemology.

  RELEVANCE: Provides direct evidence for the domain's question about what Lenin retained versus revised from Plekhanov: the 1908 reflection theory closely tracks Plekhanov's materialist epistemology, while the 1915 Hegel notebooks mark, on Bonilla's reading, a substantive philosophical departure toward a more praxis-oriented, dialectical conception. Note that this "rupture" reading is directly contested by Oittinen (2018, this domain), who denies any such break occurred -- the two papers should be read together as opposing positions in a live scholarly disagreement.

  POSITION: Textual-philosophical argument for an internal evolution/rupture in Lenin's epistemology, complicating any simple continuity narrative from Plekhanov through Lenin to Soviet diamat.
  },
  keywords = {Lenin, reflection-theory, Hegel, epistemological-break, High},
  abstract = {Este artigo discute a teoria do reflexo desenvolvida por Lenin em seu confronto com a filosofia hegeliana nos comentarios que escreveu a The Science of Logic em 1915. Nesses comentarios, Lenin apresenta a reflexao no contexto de uma relacao reflexiva adaptativa entre o movimento da o conhecimento e o movimento da realidade; a dinâmica do conhecimento segue o movimento da realidade e a influencia. Para Lenin desses comentarios, a reflexao e um momento (mental) do processo de objetificacao humana que transforma a realidade e origina o ser humano.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{antonov2021matter,
  author = {Antonov, Alexey V.},
  title = {Lenin and Empiriocritics about Matter. Is the Discussion Over?},
  journal = {Vestnik of Saint Petersburg University. Philosophy and Conflict Studies},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {37},
  number = {1},
  pages = {16--29},
  doi = {10.21638/spbu17.2021.102},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Revisits the early-twentieth-century philosophical discussion between Lenin and the "empiriocritics" (the Machist current Plekhanov and Lenin both opposed) over the definition and mode of existence of matter. Argues that Lenin considered the question only in its ontological and gnoseological aspects, and that his own principle of the identity of dialectics, logic, and theory of knowledge requires supplementing the discussion with a logical aspect, which should lead to a correction of Lenin's definition of matter.

  RELEVANCE: Engages directly with the philosophical content of the anti-empiriocritical polemic that Plekhanov initiated and Lenin systematized in "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism," testing the durability of the reflection-theoretic definition of matter that became foundational to Soviet diamat.

  POSITION: Internal philosophical critique that treats the Plekhanov/Lenin definition of matter as still-contested rather than settled orthodoxy.
  },
  keywords = {Lenin, empiriocriticism, matter, diamat, Medium},
  abstract = {The problem of matter in philosophy has become relevant again. Nowadays, it does not resemble the crisis of the early XX century when a so-called "dissolution" of the matter in mathematical formulae was discussed. However, the emergence of term "dark matter" in physics, which is not clear how it differs from the well-known "light matter", is obscure and raises the question of the "matter doubling". This makes one remember that the current definition of matter in philosophy was developed as a result of a discussion concerning the ways of its existence between Vladimir Lenin and empiriocritics. Thus, all modern attempts to define matter cannot exist without a new view on the old discussion presented in the book "Materialism and Empirio-Criticism" by Lenin. The author of the article pays attention to the fact that during the discussion of "the main question of philosophy" a century ago, concerning the attitude of matter towards consciousness, it was considered by Lenin only in two aspects, specifically, ontological and gnoseological. At the same time, the principle of the identity of dialectics, logic and theory of knowledge proposed by him demands consideration of the arguments adduced in the discussion as well as the logical aspect. According to the author, the consecutive consideration of the main question of philosophy by means of logic, should result in a correction of Lenin's definition of matter.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

