  seizure of power was contingent on the specific personality and decisions of Lenin
  in a way strict Plekhanovian determinism cannot explain away.

  RELEVANCE: Supplies a sustained non-Marxist philosophical critique of the
  determinism/individual-agency synthesis that Plekhanov's "Role of the Individual in
  History" essay proposes, independently reaching a skepticism about its coherence
  that parallels Shaw's (1988) analytic critique catalogued in the
  philosophical-foundations domain, but doing so through the historical test case of
  1917 rather than through conceptual analysis alone.

  POSITION: Classic mid-century critique of Marxist historical determinism,
  originally published in 1943 and here in a Routledge reissue of the revised
  edition, treating Plekhanov as an instance (alongside Engels and Trotsky) of
  determinism's explanatory limits.
  },
  keywords = {determinism, role-of-individual, critical-assessment, Medium},
  abstract = {The interaction of the individual in history and politics has posed major theoretical questions of historical analysis for the past two centuries: is social destiny shaped by forces beyond the power of the individual, or can the future be mastered by collective effort under the outstanding leadership of heroic men and women? In this classic study, a major philosopher and social theorist of the twentieth century offers a searching examination of the conditions under which individuals make choices that significantly alter the course of historical events and presents a scathing critique of various forms of social determinism that deny the individual freedom of action or a decisive role in history. The myth of the hero as the savior of the tribe or nation, as Hook notes, is older than written history. Until the ninteenth century, the hero functioned not merely as a cult figure but as a principle of historical explanation, a key to the rise and fall of countries and even of cultures. The exaggerations and omissions of this point of view produced an equally simplistic reaction with the formulation of determinist historiographies in which physical, racial, social, and economic forces replaced individuals as the dynamic factors in the development of events. Hook singles out orthodox Marxism as the most all-encompassing determinist system and subjects the historical thinking of Engels, Plekhanov, and Trotsky to sharp and meticulous scrutiny. Using the Russian Revolution as a test case, Hook observes that while the February 1917 Revolution was an inevitable development, the October revolution was, according to the best historical evidence, contingent upon the personality and actions of Lenin.},
  abstract_source = {openalex}
}

@article{bassin1992geodeterminism,
  author = {Bassin, Mark},
  title = {Geographical Determinism in Fin-de-siècle Marxism: Georgii Plekhanov and the Environmental Basis of Russian History},
  journal = {Annals of the Association of American Geographers},
  year = {1992},
  volume = {82},
  number = {1},
  pages = {3--22},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-8306.1992.tb01895.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Bassin argues that Plekhanov placed environmentalist principles --
  drawing significantly on the work of the geographer Friedrich Ratzel -- at the
  foundation of his social theory, while insisting this geographical determinism
  operated within, rather than against, Marx's own conceptual framework: geography
  sets the initial conditions and limited possibilities for a society's development,
  but the causally decisive factors remain the economic relations of production,
  since Bassin reads Plekhanov as denying that environmental factors alone could
  explain class dynamics or revolutionary change.

  RELEVANCE: Directly bears on this domain's coherence question by showing that
  Plekhanov's determinism was not narrowly economic but incorporated a substantial
  environmental-geographical layer, complicating any simple "vulgar economic
  determinist" characterization while raising a new question -- addressed critically
  by Peet's rejoinder -- about whether this geographical layer is compatible with
  orthodox historical materialism at all.

  POSITION: Revisionist intellectual-historical argument for reading Plekhanov's
  materialism as incorporating a substantive, Ratzel-influenced environmental
  determinism, subsequently contested by Peet (1993) in the same journal.
  },
  keywords = {geographical-determinism, historical-materialism, coherence-debate, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract},
  abstract = {Abstract Recent attempts to construct a "contextual" history of geography have refocused attention on the theories and proponents of environmental determinism, in the attempt to establish connections with the reactionary political-intellectual needs of nineteenth-century European imperialism. The present essay seeks to broaden this interpretive perspective by documenting the existence of a strong commitment to environmentalist ideas on the far left as well. The subject of the inquiry is the eminent Russian Marxist theoretician G. V. Plekhanov. It will be seen that Plekhanov placed environmentalist principles at the very foundation of his social theory, and that he did so without any doubt that they fit properly within the general conceptual framework established by Marx himself. In developing his own analysis, Plekhanov borrowed heavily from the work of contemporary geographers, in particular that of Friedrich Ratzel. As with other determinists, Plekhanov's environmentalism was essentially "ideological" in the sense that he used it toward an ulterior explanatory purpose, but this was, in his case, a purpose which had nothing to do with the project of legitimizing European domination of the globe. Rather, he used his environmentalism to explain the backwardness and social deformation of his own native Russia. Key Words: MarxMarxismG. V. Plekhanovenvironmentalismgeographical determinismmaterialismRussiaL. I. MechnikovF. RatzelF. J. Turnerfrontier hypothesis},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{peet1993critique,
  author = {Peet, Richard},
  title = {On "Geographical Determinism in Fin-de-Siècle Marxism: Georgii Plekhanov and the Environmental Basis of Russian History" by Bassin},
  journal = {Annals of the Association of American Geographers},
  year = {1993},
  volume = {83},
  number = {1},
  pages = {156--160},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1467-8306.1993.tb01927.x},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Published as a formal comment on Bassin (1992) in the same journal,
  Peet disputes Bassin's characterization of Plekhanov's engagement with
  geographical/environmental factors, part of a wider critical literature in
  which Peet has argued that environmental-determinist readings of social theory
