  POSITION: Critical rejoinder disputing Bassin's environmental-determinist reading
  of Plekhanov, representing the "economic primacy" pole of the exchange against
  Bassin's "environment-conditions-economy" reading. Specific textual argumentation
  beyond the title and exchange format could not be verified from any API and is not
  asserted here.
  },
  keywords = {geographical-determinism, historical-materialism, coherence-debate, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@incollection{carr2021fatherrussianmarxism,
  author = {Carr, Edward Hallett},
  title = {Plekhanov: Father of Russian Marxism},
  booktitle = {Studies in Revolution},
  year = {2021},
  pages = {105--119},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  doi = {10.4324/9781003251934-7},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carr's essay -- originally contributed to The Times Literary
  Supplement and collected in his 1950 volume "Studies in Revolution," here reissued
  by Routledge -- assesses Plekhanov's founding role in Russian Marxism against the
  standard of Lenin's later theoretical and practical achievement, endorsing (per
  Isaiah Berlin's contemporaneous review, cataloged separately in this domain) the
  Communist historian A. Rothstein's harsh verdict that Plekhanov's "unforgivable
  sin" was his refusal to follow his former pupil Lenin into the October Revolution.

  RELEVANCE: A foundational mid-century assessment that explicitly ranks Plekhanov
  against Lenin and finds him wanting for failing to make the leap Lenin made in
  1917; its interpretive stance -- treating history-making decisiveness as the
  standard by which theorists should be judged -- is itself criticized by Berlin as a
  symptom of Carr's broader sympathy for the "Welthistorisch individuals who make
  history" over more scrupulous but less ruthless figures like Plekhanov.

  POSITION: Cold War-era assessment privileging Lenin's revolutionary decisiveness
  over Plekhanov's theoretical consistency, read by Berlin as excessively favorable
  to Lenin at Plekhanov's expense.
  },
  keywords = {lenin-comparison, father-of-russian-marxism, cold-war-historiography, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{berlin1951carrreview,
  author = {Berlin, Isaiah},
  title = {Review of E. H. Carr, Studies in Revolution},
  journal = {International Affairs},
  year = {1951},
  volume = {27},
  pages = {470--471},
  url = {https://berlin.wolf.ox.ac.uk/published_works/singles/bib45a.pdf},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reviewing Carr's essay collection (which includes the "Plekhanov:
  Father of Russian Marxism" chapter cataloged separately in this domain), Berlin
  argues that Carr, whose earlier historical writing treated its subjects with
  ironic detachment, has by this point fallen "deeply under the spell" of the
  "Welthistorisch individuals who make history" -- above all Lenin -- and has a
  corresponding lack of sympathy for those who, through failure of will or
  circumstance, do not. Berlin singles out Carr's endorsement of A. Rothstein's
  judgment that Plekhanov's "unforgivable sin was to have refused to follow his
  former pupil" Lenin in 1917 as an example of Carr's excessively cordial siding with
  extremists and unfair harshness toward liberal or moderate figures.

  RELEVANCE: A rare instance of an eminent philosopher directly and publicly
  challenging a leading historian's harsh verdict on Plekhanov, making this review
  itself a primary document in the historiographical debate over whether negative
  assessments of Plekhanov's break with Bolshevism reflect scholarly judgment or
  ideological sympathy for the victors of 1917; directly supports this domain's key
  question of whether critical verdicts on Plekhanov are politically motivated.

  POSITION: Reflexive meta-critique of Carr's pro-Lenin historiographical framework,
  defending (by implication) Plekhanov against what Berlin sees as an unearned
  harshness rooted in Carr's own political sympathies rather than dispassionate
  assessment.
  },
  keywords = {cold-war-historiography, carr-critique, reflexive-critique, High, web-source, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

